# Snapshot files compared byte-for-byte via include_str! / insta must
# have deterministic line endings regardless of checkout platform.
# Without this, Windows core.autocrlf=true normalizes LF to CRLF on
# checkout and every snapshot assertion that compares against an
# emitter using `ptx.push_str("...\n")` fails.
*.snap text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf

# M35.2a design-only enforcement workflow counts non-comment non-blank lines
# in stub files via `grep -v -E '^\s*(//|$)' | wc -l`. On Windows checkouts
# with core.autocrlf=true, CRLF can sneak into .rs files and `\r`-only
# blank lines fail to match `^\s*$`, inflating the line count and risking
# false-positive CI fails. Pin .rs + .yml to LF to prevent this.
*.rs text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Lexer and parser goldens (crates/nsl-{lexer,parser}/tests/) render token
# spans and string-literal values of the .nsl fixtures next to them. A CRLF
# checkout shifts every span after a comment by one column and puts a `\r`
# inside triple-quoted strings and doc comments, so three goldens fail on
# windows-latest unless the fixtures are pinned to LF like the snapshots.
*.nsl text eol=lf

# models/datasets/CORPUS_MANIFEST_v2.json pins a SHA-256 of the RAW BYTES of
# each committed tokenizer, and corpus_manifest_gate.rs re-hashes the file on
# disk to prove a retrained tokenizer cannot silently invalidate every bin
# hash. That gate reads bytes, so any checkout that rewrites line endings
# changes the hash and fails it. Windows runners default to
# core.autocrlf=true, and *.json was not pinned here, so
# models/tokenizers/special_tokens.json — the only one of the three hashed
# tokenizers that contains newlines at all (22 lines; the other two are
# single-line, which is why they never failed) — checked out as CRLF and the
# gate has been red on windows-latest since at least 2026-08-26.
# All 12 tracked .json blobs are LF today and all three recorded hashes are
# the LF hashes, so this pin is a no-op on content and a fix for checkout.
*.json text eol=lf

# Committed CPDT calibration fixtures are binary safetensors; mark as such
# so git skips text-diff heuristics and CRLF normalization.
tests/fixtures/cpdt_calibration/*.safetensors binary

# docs/wiki/Environment-Variables.md is generated by `nsl env list --markdown`
# and crates/nsl-env/tests/registry_agreement.rs compares the file with the
# renderer's output. The gate normalizes CRLF before comparing, so this pin
# is belt-and-braces: it keeps the file's bytes identical to what the
# renderer emits on every platform, so a regenerate on Windows never shows
# a whole-file line-ending diff.
docs/wiki/Environment-Variables.md text eol=lf
